# 03. Core Behavioral Principle

AEGIS must be understood as an **event-driven operational system**, not a page-driven application.

The fundamental unit of behavior is:

```text
EVENT
```

not:

```text
PAGE
```

A screen displays a state.

An interaction may cause a state transition.

The state transition may cause multiple screens to update.

Therefore:

```text
ONE EVENT
   ↓
ONE AUTHORITATIVE STATE CHANGE
   ↓
MANY RELEVANT UI CONSEQUENCES
```

---

# 04. Three State Layers

AEGIS must distinguish three major categories of state.

## 4.1 Local UI State

Temporary state required only by a specific interface.

Examples:

```text
selected tab
expanded panel
active filter
hover state
modal visibility
temporary form input
sort order
chart zoom
```

Local UI state should not be treated as operational truth.

---

## 4.2 Global Application State

State required across multiple screens.

Examples:

```text
active event
selected threat
selected case
current investigation
current risk assessment
active containment context
active response context
current user
permissions
system connectivity
```

---

## 4.3 Domain / Persisted State

Operational facts that must survive navigation, refresh, or application restart.

Examples:

```text
events
threats
risk assessments
investigations
cases
transmission findings
containment actions
response actions
recovery assessments
preparedness lessons
research findings
audit records
```

---

# 05. Source-of-Truth Rule

For every piece of information, AEGIS must have one authoritative owner.

Example:

```text
Threat status
     ↓
Threat domain state
     ↓
Not individual screen state
```

A screen may display:

```text
threat.status
```

but must not maintain its own competing:

```text
localThreatStatus
```

as operational truth.

---

# 06. State Ownership Principle

The following rule applies:

> **Screens render domain state. They do not become the domain state.**

Therefore:

```text
UI
 ↓
Action
 ↓
Domain Logic
 ↓
State Store / Backend
 ↓
Updated State
 ↓
UI
```

not:

```text
Button
 ↓
Change local React variable
 ↓
Pretend the operation happened
```

---

# 07. Interaction Taxonomy

Every meaningful AEGIS interaction belongs to one of these categories.

## Read

Inspect information without changing operational state.

## Select

Choose an existing entity or context.

## Filter

Change the visible information set.

## Navigate

Move between views without changing operational truth.

## Investigate

Create or update structured investigative understanding.

## Assess

Create or modify an authorized assessment.

## Recommend

Generate or record a proposed course of action.

## Authorize

Approve an action for execution.

## Execute

Initiate or record an operational action.

## Verify

Confirm an outcome or state.

## Update

Modify an existing operational record.

## Resolve

Move an operational issue toward a terminal state.

## Learn

Convert event information into institutional knowledge.

