# 32. Real-Time State Transition

When new information arrives:

```text
External / Sensor Source
        ↓
Ingestion
        ↓
Validation
        ↓
Domain Event
        ↓
State Update
        ↓
Persistence
        ↓
Event Broadcast
        ↓
Relevant Client State
        ↓
Affected Screens Update
```

The client must not invent the new operational state independently.

---

# 33. Real-Time Conflict Resolution

If a user is editing information while a real-time update arrives:

```text
USER EDIT
+
REMOTE UPDATE
```

must not silently overwrite one another.

The implementation must define an explicit conflict strategy.

Until that strategy is defined in the engineering architecture:

> **Never silently discard user input or remote state.**

---

# 34. Optimistic Update Rule

Optimistic UI updates may only be used when the action can be safely represented as pending.

For consequential operations:

```text
User action
 ↓
Pending
 ↓
Server/domain confirmation
 ↓
Confirmed state
```

Do not present an unconfirmed consequential action as completed.

---

# 35. Failed Action Contract

When an action fails:

```text
Attempt
 ↓
Failure
 ↓
State remains valid
 ↓
User informed
 ↓
Retry where appropriate
 ↓
Audit failure where required
```

The UI must never show:

```text
SUCCESS
```

when persistence or execution failed.

---

# 36. Undo / Reversal Rules

Not every operational action should support generic undo.

For high-consequence operations, use:

```text
new compensating action
```

rather than silently rewriting history.

Example:

Instead of:

```text
Delete containment action
```

use:

```text
Containment action
 ↓
Cancelled / Superseded
```

with an audit trail.

---

# 37. State Transition Audit Model

For every consequential state change:

```text
WHO
WHAT
WHEN
WHY
FROM
TO
EVIDENCE
AUTHORITY
RESULT
```

must be available where applicable.

